      "strengths_deep_dive": [
        "United Muslim Relief demonstrates broad operational scale in global health and emergency response, delivering direct relief at a cost per beneficiary of $7.44. Independent oversight bodies confirm strong governance structures; the organization holds an <cite id=\"1\">overall score of 94.5/100 from Charity Navigator</cite> and <cite id=\"11\">meets all 20 BBB Wise Giving Alliance Standards for Charity Accountability</cite>. Furthermore, its fundraising costs remain low, spending <cite id=\"1\">only $0.02 to raise every $1 in contributions</cite>."
      ],
      "summary": "United Muslim Relief operates as an international humanitarian organization providing low-cost aid, emergency relief, and health support to marginalized communities globally. In FY2024, the organization reported <cite id=\"3\">$149,888,609 in total revenue</cite>, reflecting substantial multi-year operational growth. The charity maintains high alignment for Muslim donors through <cite id=\"2\">verified zakat-eligible programs</cite> that direct resources toward vulnerable groups across several countries, including Palestine, Lebanon, and Kenya. Third-party evaluators highlight its organizational practices, noted by an <cite id=\"1\">overall score of 94.5/100 from Charity Navigator</cite> and accreditation showing it <cite id=\"11\">meets all 20 BBB Wise Giving Alliance Standards</cite>. However, donors should evaluate the underlying financial composition. While reported tax filings list a raw program expense ratio of 47.5%, the organization relies heavily on non-cash gifts-in-kind. When evaluating cash expenditures alone, the cash-adjusted program ratio drops to 48%, meaning less than half of cash functional spending directly funds programs. Additionally, liquidity remains tight, as the charity operates with approximately <cite id=\"1\">0.3 months of working capital reserves</cite>.",
